course: dictionary
lesson: 12335
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. «Courtship» is one of them. In a science lesson it means the special behaviour animals use to attract a mate. In an old story it means the time two people spend getting to know each other before they marry.
explain: Look at the words around it. «The male peacock spread his bright tail in a courtship display.» The animal and the word display tell you this is about animals. «Their courtship lasted two years of walks and letters before they married.» Walks, letters and marrying tell you it means two people getting to know each other.
explain: A third meaning is more formal: trying hard to win someone's support or favour. «The shop gave free gifts in its courtship of new customers.» Ask yourself: who is being won over? Customers, not a mate or a marriage, so this meaning fits. Then swap your meaning into the sentence to check it.
fact: Courtship means special behaviour that animals use to attract a mate, such as dancing, singing or showing off bright feathers.
fact: Courtship can also mean the time when two people get to know each other romantically before they marry.
fact: In a formal sense, courtship means trying hard to win someone's support or favour.
fact: A courtship display is a show an animal makes to attract a mate, like a peacock spreading its tail.
fact: Some penguins give a pebble as a gift during courtship.
exercise: «The peacock performed a courtship dance for the peahen.» What does courtship mean here? | Trying hard to win a customer's favour | Time two people spend getting to know each other before marriage | A bird's nest made from twigs | *Special behaviour animals use to attract a mate
exercise: «Their courtship lasted two years of walks and letters before they married.» What does courtship mean here? | Buying a new house together | *The time two people spend getting to know each other before marriage | Trying hard to win customers | A bird's song
exercise: «The shop gave free gifts in its courtship of new customers.» Which meaning fits? | Showing off bright feathers | Two people getting married | Animals dancing to attract a mate | *Trying hard to win someone's support or favour
test: «Male bowerbirds decorate their nests during courtship.» Which meaning of courtship fits? | Time two people spend getting to know each other before marriage | Trying hard to win someone's favour | *Special behaviour animals use to attract a mate | A kind of old-fashioned dance
test: Ella says courtship always means two people falling in love. Is she right? | No, it can also mean animal behaviour or a formal effort to win support | *No, courtship can mean animal behaviour, a romantic time before marriage, or trying hard to win favour | Yes, but only in science lessons | Yes, courtship only ever means love between people
exam: Jayden reads, «The bank's courtship of young savers was very clever.» Which meaning is right, and how do you know? | Getting married, because savers are people | *Trying hard to win favour, because the bank is working to win savers | Getting to know each other, because banks are friendly places | Animal behaviour, because the bank is a kind of bird
